        "id": 81849,
        "name": "Phantom5613",
        "username": "Phantom5613",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/phantom5613/{size}/1906_2.png",
        "created_at": "2013-11-15T02:32:41.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Rabit\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/rabit/48/5774_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Rabit:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Personally, I think his physical structure was altered in a way that he can bend his head down&nbsp;without great effort, maybe rotating his shoulders around to lower his head.&nbsp;(I mean this in the best possible way:) What's especially funny to me is that, in actual comics, we see women in impossible poses that contort their bodies to ensure certain body parts are visible to the&nbsp;(generally male) reader, but we don't actually concern ourselves with that. But one guy can bend his head down and we're confused and asking questions about his anatomy.  <img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/sunglasses.png?v=12\" title=\":sunglasses:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":sunglasses:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"></p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I second this feeling.</p>",
